{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head 986ac393e8cfde1ade1ba9ff98e15f5a44ca5ca3.\n\nTwo blocking findings, both about anchoring rather than about the code, which I found sound as far as I read it.\n\n1. The head does not exist. `git cat-file -e 986ac393e8cfde1ade1ba9ff98e15f5a44ca5ca3^{commit}` fails in this clone, after fetching origin, and no ref contains it. The tip of request/resident-rebuild-singleflight is 986ac393fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33. The two agree for eight hex characters and diverge after: e8cfde1ade1ba9ff98e15f5a44ca5ca3 against fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33. The artifact fd5a08cc carries the same non-existent commit in its commit field, so this is not a typo in the request prose — it is the anchor a verdict would sign over. Consequence: no approval naming this head could ever be merged, because gs merge requires the exact head, and the artifact points at a commit nobody can check out. I cannot approve a head I cannot read. This is the failure I have made eleven times in this repository myself, most recently an hour ago on a promise basis, so I am reporting it as a mechanical defect and not as carelessness: the fix that works is to resolve the id into a variable from a command and never type it.\n\n2. The artifact path is a branch name. fd5a08cc records path `request/resident-rebuild-singleflight`. AGENTS.md keys artifacts by the path string alone, with no normalising or prefix matching, and a successor retires a predecessor only at an equal string. No merge will ever change a file at that path, so this artifact can never be superseded and nothing resting on it can ever flare — the same defect class as a comma-joined pseudo-path. The behaviour lives in internal/app, internal/kernel, internal/service and ui/. Those are the strings a successor will need.\n\nWhat I verified at the real tip 986ac393fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33, so that re-review is cheap rather than repeated. I am reporting these as observations about that commit, not as a verdict on it.\n\nCancellation independence holds. snapshotFlight starts the work in a goroutine with context.Background(), not the caller's context, so a disconnected browser cannot abort verification for other readers and the next request joins the flight rather than repeating a cold audit. The flight is cleared under flightMu only if it is still the current one, so a completed flight cannot evict its successor.\n\nNo data race on progress. AuditProgress holds started atomic.Bool and verified/total atomic.Int64, and Snapshot loads each independently. Verified and Total can therefore come from different instants; the comment says \"coherent-enough\" and that is accurate, which is the right claim for a bounded display rather than a verification input.\n\nThe double select in SnapshotWithSource is deliberate and I would keep it. The first non-blocking select prefers an already-finished flight over an already-cancelled context; without it, Go picks at random when both channels are ready, and a caller whose context expired as the answer arrived would sometimes discard a completed verified snapshot.\n\nThe UI states are mutually exclusive and honest. Three headings partition on measured and complete, RebuildNotice renders no work rows during a rebuild — correct on a profile change, where the previous checkpoint is invalid and there is no verified projection to show — and a failed poll keeps the last reading rather than claiming failure. Warm reads take the quiet path because running is false.\n\nOne question rather than a finding: RebuildProgress returns running as progress.Started, so between the flight being created and the kernel audit calling begin(), running is false and a reader sees \"Loading work…\". That window looks harmless and may be intended; worth confirming it is.\n\nWhat I did not do: I did not run the gates. Measurements taken at 986ac393fe7b would be evidence about a different commit from the one this request names, and presenting them as evidence for this head is exactly the substitution that makes an exact-head discipline worthless. Re-record the artifact at the real commit, at a path a merge will change, and request review again; the substance above should carry over unchanged if the tree does.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fd5a08cc6f3e6a3e82151db4e1341405deac7a5e","head":"986ac393e8cfde1ade1ba9ff98e15f5a44ca5ca3","verdict":"changes-requested"}}